## 🚀 Why two layers: generated and hand-written

### ⚡ Features

- **📐 Types derive from the contract**: models, enums, signatures and JSDoc all come from the OpenAPI document, so nothing drifts
- **🌐 Driven by remote documents**: fetch the URL the server exposes instead of vendoring swagger files into the repository
- **🔐 Auth-friendly**: Bearer token and custom headers for fetching, with the token read from an environment variable only
- **⏱️ Timeout and retry**: the adapter cancels on timeout and backs off exponentially, retrying only what is worth retrying
- **🧯 Unified error semantics**: every non-2xx throws `HttpError` carrying `status`, `url` and the parsed `body`
- **🪝 Pluggable hooks**: `onRequest` / `onResponse` for signing, trace IDs and telemetry
- **📦 Dual-format output**: ESM + CJS + declarations from a single build, usable by old and new projects alike
- **🚢 Release as a pipeline**: changesets owns versioning and the changelog, merging the PR publishes to npm with provenance
- **🧪 Real tests**: the hand-written layer is fully covered with a fake fetch and never touches the network

### Why the generated sources are committed

The document endpoint usually lives on an internal network that public CI runners cannot
reach. Without committed output, CI could neither typecheck nor build, which means no
quality gate at all. Committing it brings two bonuses: contract changes show up as
reviewable diffs in pull requests, and anyone can clone and build without credentials.

## ⚠️ Important conventions

| Convention | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Never hand-edit `src/generated/` | Every `pnpm generate` rewrites it wholesale, so edits are lost |
| Never bump the version by hand | changesets generates it in the release PR |
| Never commit a token | Inject it through the `[[ .TokenEnvName ]]` environment variable |
| Breaking changes need a major | Removed fields or changed types break consumers at compile time |
| Commit the lockfile | CI runs `--frozen-lockfile` and fails without it |

